 ['A59-year-old man developed sudden right-sided weakness after briefly bending over in his garden,2days after a5-hour flight. He currently smoked one pack every other day since age15; father had multiple cerebrovascular accidents and sister hemorrhagic stroke. Body hyperlipidemia versus Table1 dyslipidemiaNo remains unresolved. CT showed left frontoparietal stroke and MRI acute left-MCA infarct. Outside-hospital admission vitals/EKG/hypercoagulable workup were unknown; he later attended acute stroke rehabilitation. MRA/carotid ultrasound were negative for large-vessel occlusion/stenosis. Historical TEE notes described probable small/tiny PFO with minimal shunt and no thrombus; original report was unavailable. Echo LVEF estimate50–55%, PAP30.9mmHg normal, RAP10mmHg intermediate and LAP8.4mmHg normal. Later plain TCD3months after stroke showed no focal stenosis but holocephalic diminished systolic upstroke, distinct from unavailable bubble-TCD data.', 'Snoring/nocturnal awakenings prompted polysomnography4months after stroke: mild overall OSA with AHI6.1(denominator unstated), source-termed severe REM AHI29.6/h, oxygen nadir88% and1%total sleep time below90%. CPAP12cmH2O began5months after stroke following titration, and an IVC filter was also placed then without stated indication. At6months he self-reported8–9h/night use on100%last30nights. The first available objective report6years after stroke showed8h use on100%last30days with residualAHI0.4/h. He opted against PFO closure in favor of unnamed antiplatelet therapy. No stroke recurrence was reported to date; the figure’s15-year endpoint is retained as image data without inventing a narrative calendar date.'],
